WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.500
Horror hit factory Blumhouse has returned
to theaters with a mind-bending sequel to

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.450
Happy Death Day and this time, everything's
different.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.700
While the spacetime-twisting tale may be a
little bit headier than its predecessor, there's

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.660
no reason to be caught out of the loop.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.340
Let's break down the ending of Happy Death
Day 2U.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.360
The first Happy Death Day follows Tree Gelbman's
journey from a stuck-up sorority mean girl

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.360
to a sympathetic, take-charge survivor.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:28.160
It was a satisfying character arc, told in
charming fashion, feeling like Groundhog Day

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.000
by way of Friday the 13th.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.179
"Hey, uh, you know I don't think you should
be taking that many.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.589
You could, uh, you could die."

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.340
"If only it were that easy."

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:41.269
In the first movie, the mechanism that causes
Tree to go into a day-repeating time loop

00:00:41.269 --> 00:00:46.240
is never revealed, appearing simply as a mystical
phenomenon that serves to drive the story.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.960
But director Christopher Landon has long promised
that an explanation for the loop is hidden

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.140
in the movie, set up in advance to be explored
in the sequel.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.519
As he claimed in an interview with Insider,

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.780
"The whole idea for my sequel is actually
already in this movie.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.300
It's hiding in plain sight."

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:06.760
Many viewers took the director's lead, concocting
their own theories about the loop's origins.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:11.300
The movie's emphasis on Tree's deceased mother,
as well as their long-lost tradition of blowing

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:15.920
out birthday candles together, gave rise to
the hypothesis that Tree's mom had something

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.510
to do with the loop, with her spirit somehow
manipulating time from beyond the grave to

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:21.480
keep her daughter safe.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:27.280
But Happy Death Day 2U firmly debunks those
theories, revealing a source for the phenomenon

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.200
that's more science-fictiony in nature than
anyone saw coming.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.420
In what feels like a wild departure from the
original, Happy Death Day 2U reveals that

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:40.180
Tree's time-twisting journey was caused by
a literal time machine, located in a science

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.540
lab on the Bayfield University campus.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:47.430
The device is partially the invention of Carter's
roommate Ryan, the oafish goober from the

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:50.860
first movie with a tendency of barging in
every time Tree wakes up.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:55.980
While the first movie paints Ryan as awkward
and sex-obsessed, the sequel reveals his hidden

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:59.640
depths, as well as an intellect that would
do good by Doc Brown himself.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:04.740
The opening act of the sequel reveals that
Ryan is in the process of developing the time

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:08.060
machine with a team of fellow student scientists
named Samar and Sarah.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:12.159
While they haven't perfected the device just
yet — and who knows how they're funding

00:02:12.159 --> 00:02:16.329
this thing — the team soon discovers that
it's fully capable of disturbing the fabric

00:02:16.329 --> 00:02:17.140
of space-time.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:21.670
The time loops turn out to be an unintentional
side effect of their machine, meaning that

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:26.260
of all people, it was actually Ryan who was
responsible for Tree's most bogus birthday

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:26.760
journey.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.420
She's not happy about it.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:32.900
The lab students who developed the machine
call it the Sisyphus quantum cooling reactor,

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.220
or "Sissy" for short.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.379
The name is an on-the-nose reference to the
Greek legend of Sisyphus, a man punished by

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:44.180
the gods to spend each day rolling a boulder
up a hill, never quite reaching the top.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.960
As viewers of the first Happy Death Day know,
it's a trial that Tree can definitely relate

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:49.460
to.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.040
"I am so done with this sh-",

00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:57.560
The introduction of Sissy marks a clear pivot
from the first movie's horror trappings toward

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:59.060
straight-up science-fiction.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:05.299
But Happy Death Day 2U also leans into a more
romantic angle than its acerbic slasher predecessor,

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:08.659
becoming more of a sci-fi rom-com than the
Scream-like original.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:13.000
When the machine is activated to kick off
the movie's second act, it sends Tree into

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.909
a parallel dimension, in which her relationships
with the people around her are ever-so-slightly

00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:18.480
askew.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:23.989
The changes are small but significant, forcing
Tree to reevaluate her own life, and what

00:03:23.989 --> 00:03:25.170
she wants out of it.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:29.639
While the first dimension's Carter is single
until he meets Tree, the second dimension

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:34.660
version is happily dating Danielle, a rival
of Tree's and the alpha dog of her sorority.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:39.189
In Tree's world, her mother has been dead
for the last three years, but in the second

00:03:39.189 --> 00:03:41.820
world that she ends up in, her mother is still
alive.

00:03:42.629 --> 00:03:46.940
As a result of these change-ups, the tension
in the story this time around isn't so much

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:52.120
"will Tree survive" as it is "will love prevail,"
with the Babyface villain's killing spree

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.160
being left somewhat in the background.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.079
In the sequel, Tree's biggest challenge is
figuring out whether she wants to live in

00:03:58.079 --> 00:04:02.469
a reality with her resurrected mother, or
go back to her original dimension, where she'd

00:04:02.469 --> 00:04:06.060
just begun what could end up being a loving,
long-term relationship with Carter.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.340
Much like Scream, Cabin in the Woods, and
other winking works of meta-fiction, the characters

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:15.680
in Happy Death Day 2U are aware of the fact
that they seem to be living through the plot

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:16.280
of a movie.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:21.720
In this case, parallels are drawn with Back
to the Future Part II, the time-hopping adventure

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.460
sequel from 1989.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:28.780
That movie saw Marty McFly not just revisiting
the past, but also the plot of the previous

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:33.020
movie, intersecting with the older narrative
in new and unexpected ways.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:38.000
On top of the Back to the Future sequel being
surprisingly relevant to the movie's plot,

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.020
Happy Death Day 2U also sports a few references
to the sci-fi series.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:46.860
Examples of these tips of the hat include
a wood chipper from "Biff's Tree Removal"

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:50.980
that Tree cheerfully tosses herself into at
the end of one loop, as well as the fateful

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:55.530
speed of 88 mph she attains during one desperate
death behind the wheel.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:00.700
"When this baby hits 88 mph, you're gonna
see some serious s---."

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:05.460
While it's not necessarily hard to follow,
Happy Death Day 2U clearly doesn't want the

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:10.240
audience to linger too much on the how of
its storytelling, breezing through plot points

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:13.150
faster than you can say "wait, what?"

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:17.450
After being shot through spacetime to relive
her birthday loop in a whole other dimension,

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:19.550
Tree is faced with two tasks.

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:23.940
The first goal is to return to her own reality,
a course of action which she can't immediately

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:28.180
decide if she wants to take, considering that
her mother is still alive in this new world.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:32.580
But her second goal of stopping the time loop
is something she has to achieve no matter

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:36.280
what, regardless of which reality she decides
to stay in.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:41.700
The problem is that Sissy is a work-in-progress,
a powerful machine that neither Ryan nor his

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.900
lab mates yet entirely understand.

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:48.940
In order to get it under their control, they
need to do more research — lots of research.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:53.720
Each time the loop resets, they lose their
progress, with the memories of everyone but

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:57.040
Tree being reset to the state they were in
when the loop started.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:01.660
As a result, Tree is forced to become the
team's institutional memory, keeping track

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:05.170
of the complex quantum mechanics that make
the machine work.

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:09.920
It's a tall task and a battle of inches, with
the team only making progress thanks to Tree

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:15.210
resetting the loop by offing herself, elaborately
and hilariously, over and over again.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:16.210
The three killers

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:19.910
Complicating all of this quantum confusion
is the inconvenient fact that, just like the

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:22.320
first movie, there's a killer on the loose.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:26.620
In the first film, the Babyface masked killer
is portrayed by two characters — Tree's

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:31.330
sorority sister Lori, and a serial killer
named John Tombs, whom Lori sets up to frame

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:32.200
for Tree's death.

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:37.800
In the first movie's reality, Lori was motivated
to kill Tree out of jealousy due to her romantic

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:42.440
relationship with Gregory Butler, a married
professor and medical doctor whom Lori wanted.

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:47.740
The doe-eyed nice girl was a most unexpected
villain, making for a satisfying twist when

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.820
she's actually innocent in the second world.

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:53.140
So who's killing people in Happy Death Day
2U?

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:57.520
The sequel features three characters wielding
knives behind the Baby mask.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:02.240
For the most part, the killings in the second
world are being committed by Dr. Butler himself,

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:07.440
as well as — shockingly — his wife Stephanie,
who seems to have been driven insane by Gregory's

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:08.710
infidelity.

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:12.530
While the two at first appear to be in cahoots
with each other, that doesn't really hold

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:16.260
up after Gregory decides to shoot Stephanie
during the movie's climax.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:21.040
In this reality, they're apparently both just
crazy, and seem to totally deserve each other.

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:23.520
What a shame that it doesn't work out.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:28.400
The third killer only appears in the movie's
first act, while Ryan is experiencing his

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:32.440
own time loop on September 19th — and the
reveal is a weird one.

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:37.580
One of the weirdest threads in Happy Death
Day 2U is the idea that the Babyface killer

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:43.190
pursuing Ryan in the movie's opening is actually
another version of himself, invading the first

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:46.880
Ryan's reality to stop him from causing the
time loops in the first place.

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:51.600
It's a tantalizing reveal to kick off the
movie's second act, promising doppelgängers,

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:56.580
evil twins, and intertwining timelines that
the movie just doesn't follow up on.

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:59.120
If you thought you missed something, well,
you didn't.

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:05.080
Despite that lack of a firm explanation, the
second Ryan seems to be from the future, possessing

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:09.280
knowledge about the time loop's effects that
no one beyond Tree should really have yet.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:14.200
As much as it seems like this plot development
could be a setup for Tree's own doppelgänger

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:17.060
being the killer in dimension two, that's
not the case.

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:20.940
In fact, Tree is explicitly told that there
aren't two of her in dimension two.

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:24.600
When Tree goes to the second universe, she
replaces herself in it.

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:29.620
Despite the precedent set by a second Ryan,
an alternate version of Tree never shows up.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:33.120
So how did Ryan end up with two copies of
himself running around?

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:37.080
There are many possibilities — but in the
movie itself, there are no answers.

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:42.900
Happy Death Day 2U seems to end on a cheerful
note of Tree returning to her own reality,

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:47.020
the time loop broken, and all of her hard-earned
progress in life restored.

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:52.520
But the world of the movie gets blown wide-open
in an out-of-left-field mid-credits sequence,

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:56.220
which takes the sci-fi aspects of the sequel
and goes all the way with them.

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:01.290
The scene begins with Tree and her co-conspirators
doing campus clean-up as punishment for their

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:05.900
reality-shattering lab work, the destructive
effects of which the faculty can only begin

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:06.680
to imagine.

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:11.090
But just because the Dean is in the dark about
the time machine's capabilities doesn't mean

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:11.840
everyone is.

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:16.520
Out of nowhere, the students are approached
by a fleet of dark-suited special agent types

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:19.240
from DARPA, a real-life federal research agency.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:23.700
The agents promptly transport the students
to their headquarters, where they've set up

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:25.180
the confiscated Sissy machine.

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:29.560
It's revealed that the agency wants to do
their own research with the machine, but they

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:32.100
need the students' know-how in order to make
it work.

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:36.800
They also need a suitable test subject for
a new time loop of their own creation.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:41.790
As Carter points out, what they're proposing
is a sadistic experience that no one deserves

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:46.779
to be put through — except maybe Danielle,
who ends the movie waking up and screaming,

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:48.640
apparently caught in her own time loop.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:53.240
The ending of Happy Death Day 2U marks a wild
shift in tone that the sequel has spent its

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:58.160
whole runtime building to, setting up a third
movie in a way few could have anticipated.

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:03.600
We're not just theory-crafting, either writer-director
Chris Landon and producer Jason Blum have

00:10:03.611 --> 00:10:08.000
already said they intend to make a third movie,
with the likelihood of it happening all depending

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.820
on the box office performance of Happy Death
Day 2U.

00:10:11.770 --> 00:10:17.420
As Blum explained in an interview with CinePOP,
the genre-twisting nature of 2U is totally

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:17.920
intentional.

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:18.980
Blum said,

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:23.570
"I've never seen a franchise where one movie
is a certain genre and the next movie is a

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:24.620
different genre.

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:29.020
And that's certainly the case with Happy Death
Day and Happy Death Day 2U, and hopefully

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:32.080
if we make a third one it'll be a different,
third kind of genre."

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:37.691
While horror fans may be disappointed in the
series leaving behind its slasher roots, it's

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:40.180
hard not to be interested in where this story
could go.

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:45.890
Landon teased a potential third movie by saying
he has what feels like, quote, "a really bonkers,

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:46.580
fun idea.”

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:51.460
If audiences turn out for the weirdness of
Happy Death Day 2U, then we might get the

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:55.360
chance to see the series go full-bore nutso
in the next and final chapter.

00:10:55.940 --> 00:10:59.760
Is it possible that this movie was designed
with loose ends that can be tied up later,

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:02.600
from the mid-credits shocker to the mysterious
second Ryan?

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:06.680
It certainly seems that way — and if the
threequel gets the greenlight, we can't wait

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:08.180
to see where the story goes.

